<div style="width: 100%; padding: 20px; background-color: #e0f2f7; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; padding-bottom: 5px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin: 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's Festival Season.</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Food I Eat During a Celebration</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Express basic needs and feelings using simple phrases. Interactive: Interact in a predictable pattern with simple questions and answer about familiar things using simple phrases.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe basic needs and preferences related to festival activities using simple sentences (e.g., "I don't like loud drums."). Can ask and respond to predictable questions about festival foods and activities (e.g., "What do you eat at the festival?"). Can describe familiar activities using short, simple phrases (e.g., "I dance!").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by playing a short, upbeat festival music clip. Students stand up and clap their hands or dance to the music. The teacher then displays flashcards of festival foods (cotton candy, shaved ice, corn on the cob, meal) and activities (fireworks, dance, music) from previous lessons. The teacher points to each flashcard and says the word, encouraging students to repeat chorally (e.g., "Cotton candy!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the target phrases: "I like (cotton candy)." and "It is (fun)!" using flashcards and expressive gestures. For pronunciation, the teacher exaggerates the /f/ sound in "fireworks" and /m/ sound in "meal", having students repeat. The teacher then shows a flashcard of "party" and emphasizes the initial /p/ sound for phonemic awareness. The teacher points to a picture of "fireworks" and says, "I like the fireworks. They are bright!" The teacher then points to "cotton candy" and says, "I like cotton candy."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses simple questions to check understanding.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a picture of cotton candy) "Is this a meal or cotton candy?" (Expected answer: "Cotton candy!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a picture of fireworks) "Are the fireworks loud or quiet?" (Expected answer: "Loud!")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "It's Festival Season."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple illustrated dialogue on a large poster or whiteboard showing two children at a festival. The register is informal, and the format is a short, guided conversation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interacting verbally to exchange basic information about preferences and actions related to festival food and activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows the dialogue poster:
                        <br>Child A: "What do you like to eat at the festival?"
                        <br>Child B: "I like shaved ice!"
                        <br>Child A: "It is fun!"
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the dialogue aloud twice, pointing to each speech bubble and character.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students repeat the dialogue chorally, first as a whole class, then in two groups (one group for Child A, one for Child B).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher introduces alternative responses using other food flashcards (cotton candy, corn on the cob) and actions (dance, sing), having students repeat: "I like cotton candy!" "I like to dance!"</li>
                </ol>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "What does Child B like to eat?" (Expected answer: "Shaved ice!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to a picture of "fireworks" and asks, "Do they talk about fireworks?" (Expected answer: "No.")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice asking and answering "What do you like to eat?" and "I like..." with festival food and activity vocabulary. Grammar: WH-questions, Present Simple "I like...", Subject + verb + adjective "It is fun/loud/bright." Vocabulary: festival, celebration, meal, shaved ice, cotton candy, corn on the cob, music, dance, fireworks, fun, happy, loud, bright.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Choral Repetition & Pointing:</b> The teacher holds up various festival flashcards (e.g., "cotton candy," "shaved ice," "fireworks," "music"). The teacher says, "I like..." and students complete the sentence chorally, e.g., "I like cotton candy!" "I like fireworks! They are bright!" The teacher then says, "It is..." and students complete it, e.g., "It is fun!" or "It is loud!" (1-2 word utterances).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Q&A Drill:</b> The teacher leads a Q&A session. Teacher asks, "What do you like to eat at the festival?" and points to a student. That student responds with "I like..." followed by a food item. The teacher repeats this with several students, then expands to "What do you like to do at the festival?" (Expected answers: "I like to dance!", "I like to watch fireworks!"). The teacher emphasizes the /f/ and /m/ sounds when appropriate.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - "Ask and Show":</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of small festival food and activity picture cards. Student A asks, "What do you like to eat?" or "What do you like to do?" Student B chooses a card, shows it, and says, "I like (shaved ice)!" or "I like to (dance)!" They then swap roles. The teacher circulates, providing support and correcting pronunciation, especially for /p/ in "party" and initial sounds. (Sociolinguistic: Encourage students to mention any festival they know, e.g., "I like Fiestas Patrias and I like Halloween." when talking about things they like).</li>
                </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "It's Festival Season."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Festival Food Talk" - Students engage in a short, guided role-play/interview with a partner.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher divides the class into pairs. Each student receives a "Festival Food & Activity" worksheet with pictures of foods (shaved ice, cotton candy, corn on the cob) and activities (dance, watch fireworks, listen to music).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students take turns being the "Interviewer" and the "Festival-goer."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Interviewer asks: "Hello! What do you like to eat at the festival?" or "What do you like to do?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Festival-goer responds by pointing to a picture on their worksheet and saying: "I like (corn on the cob)!" or "I like to (dance)!" They can add an adjective if possible, "It is (yummy/fun/bright)."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students swap roles after 2-3 questions. The teacher reminds them to use clear pronunciation for /f/ and /m/ sounds.</li>
                </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Festival Food Talk" activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in the pair interaction activity, asking and answering questions about festival foods and activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist - Yes/No):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses "What do you like...?" to ask.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses "I like..." to answer.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses at least 2 target vocabulary words (e.g., cotton candy, shaved ice, dance).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation of target words is mostly intelligible (e.g., /f/ in fireworks, /m/ in meal).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Interacts collaboratively with partner.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket where they draw their favorite festival food or activity and complete the sentence: "At the festival, I like ________. It is _________."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words about festivals did you like to say today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to ask your friend about their favorite festival food?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The "Ask and Show" pair activity in Stage 3 and "Festival Food Talk" in Stage 4 worked well to encourage interactive speaking at the Pre-A1.4 level. Most students were able to form "I like..." and "What do you like?" phrases. The use of flashcards and realia was critical for engagement. Some students struggled with adding adjectives (e.g., "It is bright!"). For the next lesson, I will ensure more explicit practice of adjective usage with corresponding visuals and clearer sentence frames to support their descriptive output.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on speaking about festival foods and activities. In Lesson 4, students will use these same vocabulary words and sentence structures to practice writing short sentences about what they like to eat and do at a festival, moving from oral production to written composition.</p>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>